LOGBOOK.md-295-### 1824 — libssh2 adapter preserves operation scope and nonblocking argument identity (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
LOGBOOK.md-296-- ROOT CAUSE: Channel read/write timeout cancellation shut down the shared socket bridge; channel opens rejected `rekeying` before entering the session gate and started their deadline only after gate admission; each rekey caller owned the connection-global KEX; and concurrent writes could resume libssh2's single per-channel write state with a different payload after `EAGAIN`. Credential results were checked against factory capabilities but not the caller-provided allowlist.
LOGBOOK.md-297-- REWORK: Socket-service cancellation now retires only its independently cancellable readiness operation. Channel opens may queue behind rekey under their original bounded deadline. One bounded shared rekey flight coalesces trigger reasons and lets a canceled caller detach while KEX continues. Per-channel FIFO gates retain exact write arguments across `EAGAIN`, with queued bytes still reserved against the caller cap. Returned credential algorithms must belong to both adapter capabilities and the request allowlist.
LOGBOOK.md-298-- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic loopback regressions prove canceled and timed-out reads leave sibling channels and the session ready; an open times out while rekey is deliberately suspended and a second queued open succeeds afterward; two rekey triggers produce one KEX while the first caller cancels; four concurrent same-channel payloads survive forced bridge backpressure byte-exactly; and a supported-but-disallowed credential algorithm fails before authentication.
