LOGBOOK.md-778-- FAILURE OWNERSHIP: One runtime generation owns one active endpoint and one reusable TCP connection epoch. EOF, timeout, framing/correlation, partial-write ambiguity, channel, or SSH failure atomically retires the epoch, classifies all in-flight queries, and permits one coordinator to replay only eligible QUERY operations as one ordered batch at the next endpoint. Cancelled/expired/terminal work does not retry; late old-epoch callbacks cannot answer; promotion clears cache/transport generation. A valid response including any RCODE never shops resolvers.
LOGBOOK.md-779-- M2 BOUNDARY: An eligible client-UDP query gets at most one relay UDP attempt at the active endpoint. Only TC=1, pre-response size inability, UDP timeout, or typed relay failure permits M2 to request same-endpoint TCP; M1 then owns TCP reuse and later endpoint promotion. Structural work is one UDP plus at most one TCP attempt per configured endpoint under one injected deadline. Client TCP goes directly to M1; malformed/mismatched UDP does not select another resolver.
LOGBOOK.md-780-- SAFETY/PLANNING: Resolver/policy validation and SSH `direct-tcpip` readiness precede route/DNS settings. Post-settings ordinary queries have only the virtual ingress -> authenticated SSH/relay -> explicit endpoint path. Endpoint exhaustion returns bounded SERVFAIL, withdraws safe-DNS capability, and clears settings; it cannot call the device resolver. Missing legacy resolver data yields `requiresResolverConfiguration` with no inferred migration. TASK-260721-33o8fc owns schema/codec/migration, TASK-260721-2raag7 owns M4 profile experience, and revised TASK-260715-5o6jqg/TASK-260715-28jdml/TASK-260715-336ljl own shared-failure, M2, and integration evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:781:- EVIDENCE: `.research/260721_task-260715-1tnjlu-exit-dns-resolver-policy.md` compares all four policies and cites RFC 1035/7766/8484/4035/5952 plus RFC 4254 exit-side `direct-tcpip` semantics and accepted project contracts. `diagrams/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_dns-policy-flow.dot` renders and was visually inspected after the coordinated retry/M2 update. ADR-022 remains proposed until independent reviewer acceptance; no human vendor/product decision remains.
